# Art Direction – LaunchPad
- Style: clean, friendly flat-shaded 2D illustration based on the main space center background (`assets/simple-space-center.webp`): a quiet Cape Canaveral night scene with one Mission Control building at left, one launch tower at right, and a clear gate road between them. This flat-shaded 2D illustration style is used consistently throughout the game (compound, Mission Control interior `assets/illustrated-mission-control.webp`, space views `assets/illustrated-space-background.webp`, and celestial bodies `assets/illustrated-earth.webp`).
- Characters: use the player's supplied pixel sheets directly: `assets/art.jpeg` for the eight open-helmet crew portraits, `assets/more-art.jpeg` for closed visors, `assets/whatsapp-image-2026-11-14-at-14-39-38-1.jpeg` for civilian preview, and `assets/textures.jpeg` for the chicken astronaut. Crop the 4×2 sheets to individual figures in canvas, preserving nearest-neighbor edges. The captain stays anchored in the top-left HUD.
- Interaction: mouse-only polygon hotspots tightly aligned to Mission Control and the pad; keep the road, gate, and open lawn clear. Avoid extra buildings or ambient clutter.
- Delivery convoy: finalizing a mission sends a single low-loader transport truck along the access road through an opening gate to the launch pad, where umbilicals and arms connect to the rocket mount.
- Mission Control: immersive NASA operations room with illustrated consoles and personnel in headsets, large display screens (vehicle camera, dotted trajectory route map, and flight director/captain log) on the wall.
- Fonts: use Pixelify Sans throughout the game, including display text, body copy, mission-report headers, and text drawn on canvas. Keep interface copy comfortably readable at 12px minimum, with body and decision text larger; avoid tiny all-caps labels.
